 "title": "Hard vs ablative antifouling: why one hull stays clean",
 "script": "Two boats, same marina. A year later, one hull is clean, and the other is covered in barnacles. The difference? The antifouling.\n\nAntifouling is the special paint below the waterline. It slowly releases a biocide, usually copper, that stops weed and barnacles from settling.\n\nAblative paint works like a bar of soap. As water flows past, the surface slowly wears away, exposing fresh biocide. No build-up, and it keeps working after time out of the water.\n\nHard paint stays put. It dries to a tough film that leaches biocide from inside. You can scrub it and polish it smooth, so fast boats love it. But the layers build up, and eventually they must be stripped.\n\nSo, a cruiser that's hauled out or dry-stored often? Ablative. A fast boat, or a diver cleans the hull often? Hard.\n\nWhich one is on your boat? Tell us in the comments, and follow Creative Tracks Marine for more.",
